Fake system headers, so that the sources only a Mac can build can at least be
syntax checked anywhere.

src/mac/t150menu.m and src/t150d/hid_darwin.c need Cocoa and IOKit, which no
build machine here has. Until this existed the only check those files had was
being read, and reading does not find what -Werror finds: an unused parameter
left behind by a change is a hard failure of the macOS job, and therefore of
the release that job builds, and it reached that job once.

These headers declare only the types, methods and constants those two files
actually name, and they do nothing at all. What "make check-mac" proves is that
the files parse and are warning clean under the project's own flags. It proves
nothing whatever about behaviour.

Adding to them is meant to be done from the compiler's own errors, one at a
time. The one rule that matters: when clang says a selector is not declared,
check the real API before declaring it here. A stub written to match the code
rather than the framework turns this check into a mirror, which is worse than
having no check, because it passes.

Not covered: src/mac/bootswitch.c, whose IOUSBLib plug-in interfaces and
IOUSBDevRequestTO would have to be guessed at, and a wrong guess gives a false
answer. It is compiled by CI and by any Mac.
